Q: Implementation attempt of MPSC queue using atomics A: <p>Consider these lines:</p>
<pre><code>auto tail_ptr = m_tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
if (tail_ptr != nullptr) {
    tail_ptr-&gt;next.store(node, std::memory_order_release);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Two threads can run this at the same time, so both will overwrite <code>tail_ptr-&gt;next</code>. You cannot atomically change <code>tail_ptr-&gt;next</code> and <code>m_tail</code> at the same time. The common solution is to instead push nodes to the head of the queue. This way, you can prepare a new node that has <code>next</code> pointing to the current <code>m_head</code>, and then you compare-exchange <code>m_head</code> with the new <code>node</code>. If that succeeded, you're done. If not, <code>m_head</code> was changed by another thread, and you have to first update <code>next</code> to the new value of <code>m_head</code> before trying to update the latter.</p>
<p>Of course, this now introduces a problem for <code>pop()</code>, because to be a FIFO queue you then need to pop the tail, and then you need to update the second-to-last element's <code>next</code>, which again you cannot do both at the same time atomically.</p>
<p>A possible solution is to pop from the front as well, but that turns it into a LIFO queue. Another solution is to have <code>pop()</code> atomically swap the current queue with an empty one, and return the old queue. That way, the consumer can process the old queue at its own pace, and producers will push to the new queue.</p>

Q: How do I determine the number of lines in a file efficiently? A: <p>You could do</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>//const value, no need to cast it on every newlines_in_file call.
var newline_char = []byte(&quot;\n&quot;)

//don't use interfaces unless you want to use different sources(file, network, etc).
func newlines_in_file(r *os.File) (lines int, err error) {
    var (
        n   int
        buf = [256]byte{} //stack allocated buffer
    )

    //set the condition on the loop body
    for err == nil {
        n, err = r.Read(buf[:])
        lines += bytes.Count(buf[:n], newline_char)
    }

    //if you can, don't use if, switches inside loops, this one can be outside
    if err != io.EOF {
        return lines, err
    }

    //happy path is the last statement
    return lines, nil
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Doesn't use io.Reader, on the other hand 0 allocation as it uses a stack buffer.</li>
</ul>
